 <a id="TOC_3."></a>
 
@@ -315,3 +315,78 @@ When launching `android` type VMs, the following occurs, in order:
 - The Android Emulator process is started with the appropriate SDK environment
 - A QMP connection is established for lifecycle management
 - Handlers are created to watch the emulator state and handle shutdown
+
+<a id="TOC_4.4."></a>
+
+### Stub VMs
+
+The `stub` type is an experimental VM type for testing minimega itself and the
+tools that drive it, such as phenix, on hosts where QEMU/KVM and containers are
+unavailable or too slow, for example in continuous integration. A stub VM needs
+no hypervisor: it follows the normal VM lifecycle and state machine, creates
+real network taps on the configured bridges, appears in `vm info` with its
+tags, and offers the miniccc backchannel, but it runs no QEMU process and no
+container.
+
+Stub VMs reuse the `container` configuration parameters:
+
+- `init` - Optional host command, started as a stand-in for the guest when the VM is started.
+- `hostname` - Informational only.
+- `filesystem`, `preinit`, `fifo`, and `volume` - Ignored.
+
+When a stub VM is started, the `init` command runs on the host with the
+instance directory as its working directory and its output in `stub.log` in
+that directory. `vm stop` and `vm start` pause and resume the process with
+`SIGSTOP` and `SIGCONT`, and `vm kill` kills its process group. If the process
+exits on its own, the VM moves to the `QUIT` state. The process receives these
+environment variables in addition to minimega's own:
+
+- `MM_STUB_NAME` - The VM name.
+- `MM_STUB_UUID` - The VM UUID.
+- `MM_STUB_NAMESPACE` - The VM's namespace.
+- `MM_STUB_INSTANCE` - The VM instance directory.
+- `MM_STUB_CC` - The path of the cc unix socket for this VM.
+
+When `vm config backchannel` is enabled (the default), minimega listens for
+miniccc on the `MM_STUB_CC` unix socket after the VM is started. Running the
+real `miniccc` binary as the stand-in therefore makes `cc exec` and the other
+`cc` commands work end to end. minimega does not expand environment variables
+in `vm config init`, so wrap miniccc in a script. The socket is created just
+after the stand-in process starts, so the script should wait for it instead of
+relying on miniccc's 15-second reconnect interval:
+
+```bash title="/usr/local/bin/stub-miniccc.sh"
+#!/bin/sh
+while [ ! -S "$MM_STUB_CC" ]; do sleep 0.2; done
+exec /opt/minimega/bin/miniccc -family unix -parent "$MM_STUB_CC" -uuid "$MM_STUB_UUID" -path "$MM_STUB_INSTANCE/miniccc"
+```
+
+```text
+vm config net LAN
+vm config init /usr/local/bin/stub-miniccc.sh
+vm launch stub node[0-2]
+vm start all
+cc exec hostname
+```
+
+Without `init`, a stub VM is a pure state machine with taps, which is enough to
+exercise `vm launch`, `vm start`, `vm stop`, `vm kill`, `vm flush`, `vm info`,
+`vm tag`, and `vm net` without any guest.
+
+<a id="TOC_4.4.1."></a>
+
+#### Technical details
+
+When launching `stub` type VMs, the following occurs, in order:
+
+- A new VM handler is created within minimega, which is populated with a copy of the VM configuration
+- An instance directory for the VM is created (by default /tmp/minimega/N, where N is the VM ID), and the configuration is written to disk
+- Checks are performed to ensure there are no naming or UUID conflicts
+- Any specified network taps are created and attached to openvswitch, exactly as for `kvm` type VMs
+- A handler is created to watch the stand-in process and kill it on demand
+- minimega returns control to the user with the VM in the BUILDING state
+
+`vm screenshot` returns a small solid-colour PNG so that tools which collect
+screenshots of every VM still receive an image. `vm save`, `vm migrate`,
+`vm qmp`, `vm cdrom`, `vm hotplug`, `vm console`, and VNC are not available
+for stub VMs because they rely on QEMU or a container.
